A test slice is a specialized Spring Boot test configuration that loads only the beans relevant to a specific layer of your application, rather than starting the entire context. Spring Boot provides several built-in slices out of the box.

@WebMvcTest loads only the web layer: controllers, filters, ControllerAdvice, and the MockMvc infrastructure. It does NOT load service beans or repository beans — you have to mock those with @MockBean. This gives you a real Spring MVC context for HTTP testing without the cost of starting the whole application.

@DataJpaTest loads only the JPA layer: your repositories, entity classes, and an embedded H2 database by default. It doesn't load controllers or services. This is perfect for testing custom JPQL queries or repository methods in isolation.

@JsonTest loads only Jackson configuration — object mappers and serializers — so you can test JSON serialization and deserialization of your DTOs without any web or database overhead.

The benefit is that each slice starts faster than a full context because it only needs to configure a small subset of your beans. It also makes your tests more focused: a failure in a @WebMvcTest clearly points to a controller problem, not a service or database issue. Test slices are one of Spring Boot's most practical features for writing maintainable integration tests.
